course: dictionary
lesson: 1615
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one the writer means. The word chemist has two. Read the words around it to find the right one.
explain: «Worms are common in children and are easily treated with medicine from a doctor or chemist.» Medicine is near the word, so a chemist here must be a person who sells medicines. Try the meaning in the sentence: does it still make sense? It does.
explain: «Linus Pauling was a great chemist with two Nobel Prizes.» Nobel Prizes are for top scientists, so this chemist is a scientist who studies chemistry. The clues point to a different meaning, so always check the words around the word first.
fact: A chemist is a scientist who studies chemistry: what substances are made of and how they change.
fact: In British English, a chemist is also a person who prepares and sells medicines.
fact: «The chemist's» is the shop where you buy medicines, plasters and toothpaste.
fact: Americans say «drugstore» or «pharmacy» for this shop, and «pharmacist» for the person who works there.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «Worms are common in children and are easily treated with medicine from a doctor or chemist.» What does chemist mean in this sentence? | A scientist who mixes substances in a lab | *A person who prepares and sells medicines | A type of cake | A teacher of maths
exercise: «Herbal pills can do harm, so ask a pharmacist (the medicines expert at the chemist's) before taking one.» What is the chemist's here? | *The shop where you buy medicines | A science lab at school | A bus stop | A football pitch
exercise: Maya says, «My uncle is a research chemist who studies what substances are made of.» What does chemist mean? | A pilot who flies planes | A person who sells medicine | A doctor who looks after teeth | *A scientist who studies chemistry
test: Ellie says, «Mum, we need to go to the chemist's for plasters.» Where are they going? | *To a shop that sells medicines and plasters | To a school for scientists | To the seaside | To a lab full of test tubes
test: «Linus Pauling was a great chemist with two Nobel Prizes.» What does chemist mean here? | A shop assistant | A person who prepares medicines | A doctor | *A scientist who studies what substances are made of
exam: Jess says, «The chemist on the corner sells plasters.» Which meaning does she use? | A kind of teacher | A football player | A scientist who works in a lab | *A shop that sells medicines and plasters
